 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Thin Features:** Supervision from Gaussian-rendered depth alone blurs thin parts (e.g., mug handles, animal legs) because narrow structures are averaged away by soft Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. Clean distance supervision (from sphere-traced neural surfaces or ray-casted meshes) is required to recover these features [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Compact Objects:** For compact objects where Gaussian depth already captures geometry, clean distance supervision may not help and can hurt if the sphere-traced point cloud is noisy, causing the surface to "fatten" [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Learning from Images:** Directly learning from volume-rendered color loss does not recover thin parts because most sampled rays land on the background, making the path from color to distance output weak [S1, unofficial]. Step-by-step tracing supervision is unstable due to sequential gradient issues [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Ambient Occlusion Quality:** Ambient occlusion (21.3 dB) is weaker than shadows (30.3 dB) because averaging many rays accumulates small per-ray errors [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Ray Origin Bias:** The oracle is accurate primarily for ray origins on or near the surface [S1, unofficial]. Rays starting far from any surface (e.g., on a ground plane below an object) may report occlusion too often [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Thin Feature Loss:** Supervising solely on 3DGS-rendered depth blurs thin parts (e.g., mug handles) because narrow structures are averaged away by soft Gaussians. Clean distance supervision (from neural surfaces or meshes) is required to recover these features [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Compact Objects:** For compact objects where Gaussian depth already captures geometry, clean distance supervision provides little benefit and may hurt if the auxiliary surface is noisy, causing "fattening" [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Direct Image Supervision:** Learning directly from volume-rendered color loss does not help recover thin parts because most rays land on background, making the gradient signal from color to distance weak [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Step-by-Step Tracing:** Supervising sequential ray-marching steps from images is unstable due to gradient issues in the sequential loop [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Ambient Occlusion Accuracy:** AO quality (21.3 dB) is lower than shadow quality (30.3 dB) because averaging many rays accumulates small per-ray errors [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Ray Origin Dependency:** The oracle is accurate primarily for ray origins on or near the surface. Rays starting far from the surface (e.g., ground plane below an object) may report false occlusion [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Constant time per query ($O(1)$), independent of the number of Gaussians $N$ [S1, unofficial]. Each query requires a single forward pass through the network [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Constant memory ($O(1)$), fixed at ~52 MB, independent of $N$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per ray query. The DDF requires a single forward pass regardless of scene size [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ with respect to the number of Gaussians $N$. The field size is fixed at ~52 MB [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Performance:**
-    *   **Speed:** 26 to 72 times faster than sphere tracing an equivalent Signed Distance Field (SDF) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   **Scaling:** Unlike a BVH over Gaussians, whose memory grows at 336 bytes per Gaussian and latency rises by ~9x from 5K to 1M Gaussians, the DDF remains flat [S1, unofficial]. The BVH crosses 52 MB memory at ~155k Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-    *   **Hardware:** Does not require RT-core hardware [S1, unofficial]. On small scenes, RT-core BVH is faster per ray, but at real scene scales (hundreds of thousands to millions of Gaussians), the DDF becomes competitive or faster, especially with CUDA-fused kernels (tinycudann) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   **Comparison to Rasterization:** For sparse queries (1K-10K rays), the DDF is 600+ times faster than re-rasterizing with gsplat [S1, unofficial]. At full frame (1M rays), it ties rasterization (4.81 ms vs 4.45 ms) because rasterization is bound by frame buffer writing [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Quality:** Reproduces reference ray-traced shadows at 30.3 dB and ambient occlusion at 21.3 dB across 142 objects and real captured scenes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Constant time and memory, no mesh required, works with moving scenes, differentiable queries for inverse rendering [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Ambient occlusion quality is lower than shadows because averaging many rays accumulates small per-ray errors [S1, unofficial]. Accuracy degrades for ray origins far from the surface [S1, unofficial]. The method relies on the quality of the supervision source (neural surface or mesh) for thin geometry [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Speed:** 26 to 72 times faster than sphere tracing an equivalent neural SDF [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Scaling:** Unlike BVH-over-Gaussians, latency does not grow with $N$. BVH latency rises ~9x from 5K to 1M Gaussians, while DDF remains flat [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Comparison to BVH:** On small scenes (< few thousand Gaussians), RT-core BVH is faster per ray. At real scene scales (hundreds of thousands to millions of Gaussians), DDF becomes competitive or faster, especially with CUDA-fused kernels (tinycudann) [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Comparison to Rasterization:** For sparse queries (1K-10K rays), DDF is 600+ times faster than re-rasterizing. At full frame (1M rays), DDF ties rasterization (4.81 ms vs 4.45 ms) because rasterization is bound by frame buffer writing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Quality:**
+    *   **Shadows:** Reproduces reference ray-traced shadows at 30.3 dB [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Ambient Occlusion:** Reproduces reference AO at 21.3 dB [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Geometry:** Chamfer distance improves from 0.082 to 0.065 (21% drop) with clean distance supervision [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Constant time and memory, no mesh required, no RT-core hardware dependency, supports arbitrary rays.
+*   **Limitations:** AO quality is lower than shadows; accuracy degrades for ray origins far from the training surface; relies on the quality of the auxiliary geometric supervision (neural surface or mesh) for thin features [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Directed Distance Field for Gaussian Splatting was introduced to address the limitation of 3DGS rasterization, which only supports primary camera rays and cannot efficiently trace secondary rays for global illumination [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on the multiresolution hash grid encoding technique from tinycudann [S1, unofficial]. It enables a mesh-free pipeline for global illumination in Gaussian Splatting scenes by distilling the scene geometry into a compact neural field [S1, unofficial].
+The Directed Distance Field for Gaussian Splatting was introduced to address the limitation of 3DGS rasterization, which cannot trace secondary rays for global illumination [S1, unofficial]. The method distills a trained 3DGS scene into a small neural field (52 MB) that acts as a ray oracle [S1, unofficial]. It builds on multiresolution hash grid encoding techniques from tinycudann [S1, unofficial]. The approach enables mesh-free global illumination pipelines, allowing for shadows and ambient occlusion in Gaussian Splatting scenes without explicit surface reconstruction [S1, unofficial].
 
